Great question. With placeholder values you can't send real emails or verify a real domain, but you can still validate most of the stack. Here's a practical approach layered from quickest to most thorough:

1. Terraform validation (no AWS credentials needed)

```bash
cd contact-form-backend
terraform init
terraform fmt
terraform validate
```

This catches syntax errors, missing references, and type mismatches in your HCL. It won't actually talk to AWS, so placeholders are fine.

2. Terraform plan with placeholder values

```bash
terraform plan -var-file="terraform.tfvars"
```

Using your placeholder `terraform.tfvars`, this will attempt to resolve the Route 53 data source and fail because `example.com` isn't in your account. To work around that, you can temporarily comment out the `data "aws_route53_zone"` block and the DNS records that reference it, then run plan again. This lets you verify the resource graph, IAM policies, and Lambda configuration are internally consistent.

3. Test the Lambda handler locally with [FILE_1].js

Create a small test harness next to `[FILE_3].js`:

```js
// [FILE_5].js
const { handler } = require('./exports');

// Simulate the environment variables Terraform would set
process.env.SES_TEMPLATE_NAME = 'ContactFormSubmission';
process.env.SES_REGION        = 'us-east-1';
process.env.SENDER_EMAIL      = '[EMAIL_1]';
process.env.PRIMARY_RECIPIENT = '[EMAIL_2]';
process.env.ADMIN_RECIPIENT   = '[EMAIL_3]';
process.env.RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY = '6LeIxAcTAAAAAGG-vFI1TnRWxMZNFuojJ4WifJWe'; // Google's test key

async function run() {
  // -- Test 1: missing fields → expect 400 --
  const missingFields = await handler({
    body: JSON.stringify({ firstName: 'Jane' }),
  });
  console.log('Test 1 (missing fields):', missingFields.statusCode, JSON.parse(missingFields.body));

  // -- Test 2: invalid email → expect 400 --
  const badEmail = await handler({
    body: JSON.stringify({
      firstName: 'Jane', lastName: 'Doe',
      email: 'not-an-email', subject: 'Test',
      message: 'Hello', recaptchaToken: 'abc',
    }),
  });
  console.log('Test 2 (bad email):', badEmail.statusCode);

  // -- Test 3: valid payload with Google's test key → captcha passes, SES fails --
  const fullPayload = await handler({
    body: JSON.stringify({
      firstName: 'Jane', lastName: 'Doe',
      email: '[EMAIL_4]', subject: 'Test',
      message: 'Hello world', recaptchaToken: 'test-token',
    }),
  });
  console.log('Test 3 (full payload):', fullPayload.statusCode, JSON.parse(fullPayload.body));
}

run();
```

Run it with:

```bash
node [FILE_5].js
```

A few things to note about this:

• Google provides a test secret key (`6LeIxAcTAAAAAGG-vFI1TnRWxMZNFuojJ4WifJWe`) that always passes verification. That's what the harness uses, so the reCAPTCHA step will succeed without a real site.

• Test 3 will pass reCAPTCHA, but then return a 500 from SES because there's no real AWS session or verified identity. That's the expected outcome — it proves your validation and captcha logic work, and that the SES call is correctly attempted.

• Tests 1 and 2 should return 400 without ever reaching the network.

4. Test with real AWS credentials but sandbox SES

If you want to go further, use a real domain you control and keep SES in sandbox mode (which is the default for new accounts). In sandbox mode SES only delivers to verified addresses, so it's safe to experiment. You'd verify two personal email addresses, point the variables at them, deploy the stack, and curl the API Gateway URL:

```bash
curl -X POST "$(terraform output -raw api_url)" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "firstName": "Test",
    "lastName": "User",
    "email": "[EMAIL_5]",
    "subject": "Smoke test",
    "message": "End-to-end check",
    "recaptchaToken": "TOKEN_FROM_BROWSER"
  }'
```

For the captcha token in this scenario, the quickest approach is to embed the reCAPTCHA widget on a minimal local HTML page, complete the challenge, grab the token from the browser console, and paste it into the curl command within its two-minute validity window.

The short version: `terraform validate` + create `[FILE_5].js` with Google's test key gets you a solid confidence level without needing any real AWS resources or verified domains.

